294:### 3.5 Clear pieces run as loops
295-
296-While the unclear spots are being resolved, the pieces that are already clear and not blocked run as loops: a
297-defined output, the agent attempts, the check runs, pass → close the loop, fail → the feedback goes back to the agent.
298-
299-A piece is loop-ready only if it has all five:
300-
301-1. A check. Best is a script; next a fixed checklist judged by a separate agent; last EJ's own judgment.
302-2. A limit on the **number** of attempts — a count, not a time limit (time is 3.8's business).
303-3. Specific feedback: the script's real output, not just "failed".
304-4. An exit for when the limit is hit. If the attempts ran on a cheap tier, the exit is a fresh node on a stronger
305-   tier with a short handoff note (the piece, its check, the last feedback) and its own limit — never a tier switch
306-   inside the running session: a switch voids the prompt cache, so the stronger tier re-reads the whole context at
307-   its fresh input rate, and a session in which the cheap tier flailed is mostly wrong turns that would anchor the
308-   stronger tier towards the same dead ends. When that limit is hit too, or there is no stronger tier, the piece
309-   goes back to the unclear list or to EJ. It was not as clear as it looked.
310-5. A check the worker cannot edit, and that is known to be able to fail (the house sabotage check).
311-
